  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 225 AM MDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nCurrent radar shows ongoing isolated rain showers along the high \nterrain, with a few of them spilling onto the plains. These showers \ndo have the ability to produce gusty outflow winds, as surface \nobservations have already reported gusts up to 50 mph in the \nfoothills (40 mph around the Boulder area). With the passing of a \nweak upper level shortwave, additional showers and thunderstorms are \npossible before sunrise, mainly for the northern mountains, with \ngusty outflow winds as the main concern. \n\nWith northwest flow aloft today, our source for monsoon moisture \nwill be cut off. However, isolated showers and thunderstorms are \nstill expected this afternoon. CAMs favor storms forming over the \nfoothills and I-25 corridor and trekking east through the afternoon, \nwhere they will tap into better moisture, and thus better \ninstability. Most storms are expected to be marginal, but can't rule \nout isolated severe thunderstorms producing 60 mph winds and hail, \nespecially for the eastern plains where SPC has introduced a \nMarginal Risk (1/5). In addition, with PWs still over an inch, heavy \nrainfall is possible with strongest storms. However, flash flooding \nis not much of a concern, given faster storm motions. Overnight \nThursday, a weak backdoor front will bring 'cooler' temperatures for \nFriday, with forecasted highs expected to be in the 80s to low 90s.\n\nThe shortwave embedded in the upper level ridge will bring another \nround of isolated showers and thunderstorms on Friday. Modeled Skew-\nT soundings show more favorable shear profiles, but instability \nseems to be lacking. Denver metro and areas south have been included \nin a Marginal Risk (1/5) for severe weather, with hail and strong \noutflow winds as the main concern.\n\nGuidance is in good agreement of the upper level ridge \nrestrengthening once again over the weekend, with above normal \ntemperatures returning to the region. Ensemble solutions continue to \nindicate highs reaching up to 100 both Sunday and Monday for the \nplains. After that, models begin diverging as multiple upper level \nshortwaves arrive next week. Depending on the evolution of these \ndisturbances, we could be seeing 'cooler' temperatures by Tuesday \n(unfortunately it would be short lived), with precipitation \nchances returning by midweek. However, it is too soon to go into \nthe details yet as things could still change.",
